      "name": "What is the difference between humectants, emollients, and occlusives in skincare formulation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Humectants, emollients, and occlusives represent the three core categories of moisturising ingredients, each operating by a different physical mechanism to improve skin hydration. Humectants are highly hydrophilic molecules containing hydroxyl groups that bind water molecules from the surrounding atmosphere or the deeper layers of the dermis, drawing them into the stratum corneum; examples include glycerin, hyaluronic acid, and sodium PCA. Emollients are hydrophobic lipids, esters, or silicones that fill the gaps between desquamating corneocytes, smoothing the skin surface and restoring the lipid barrier; examples include squalane, isopropyl myristate, and caprylic/capric triglycerides. Occlusives are highly hydrophobic materials that form a physical, semi-permeable film over the skin surface to significantly retard transepidermal water loss (TEWL); examples include petrolatum, shea butter, and dimethicone. A balanced formulation integrates all three categories to optimize both immediate skin feel and long-term hydration." }
