    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does pH affect the stability of skin care active ingredients?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The chemical stability and biological efficacy of active ingredients are highly dependent on the pH of the formulation environment. Acidic actives like Alpha Hydroxy Acids (AHAs, e.g., glycolic acid) require a low pH to remain un-ionized and penetrate the skin barrier effectively, typically in the range of pH 3.5 to 4.0. In contrast, sensitive actives like L-ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) are highly susceptible to oxidation and degradation at higher pH ranges, requiring a very low pH below 3.5 to maximize stability, often requiring anhydrous bases to prevent hydrolysis. Retinoids are most stable in a near-neutral pH range of 6.0 to 7.0, as acidic environments accelerate their isomerisation and degradation. Formulating multi-active serums requires careful buffer selection, such as citric acid/sodium citrate or lactic acid/sodium lactate, to lock the pH within the narrow window where the selected actives remain chemically stable and biologically active." }
